mattias you took part in a panel  discussion today about do we really need  fiber now before you tell me your answer  tell me about your organization and then  the main points that you were making to  that panel okay i am the chairman of  cable europe which is basically the  european organization of all cable  operators like upc like kabul  deutschland like virgin so we are  assembling the interests of the cable  industry in in europe  and coming back to your question do we  really need fiber i think fiber is  everywhere you know even the cable  networks have a lot of fiber in their  backbones and fiber obviously will go  very closer to the house incrementally  because the development of the download  of broadband traffic will need that you  know but the cable industry does it  incrementally and we only discuss the  very very last mile  and in the very last mile the copper  network which cable has  is like a kind of a gold mine  because our technicians our engineers  invented a standard called boxes i don't  go to the details  who is able now and i just got some news  about it to develop gigabytes  symmetrical both ways over this last  mile in cable  so i think if there is a cable network  and that's in 50  of the european households we take  advantage of that because we could  immediately offer people already now 500  megabits with the old standard with a  new standard we could offer them  gigabits so we think this system this  hybrid system will be fit for a long  time fit for purpose and the advantages  you can incrementally invest you don't  need this big money of investment at a  time to  to take up a fiber network to the house  you know and the customer doesn't notice  he has the same kind of quality the same  kind of experience and service so that's  the only thing we're discussing and if  there would be no cable network  i think even cable operators would sink  in a green field to invest directly in  fiber to the home maybe maybe but it's  up to the operators to do that but it  would be a big mistake to underestimate  the value for the existing cable  infrastructure in the last mile and  while all this is going on what's your  view on the regulatory situation at the  moment  it hasn't been that bad because it's  encouraged especially infrastructure  based competition  and cable our members are one of the  main driving force for high quality  broadband in europe  and our members benefit from this  infrastructure competition and in areas  where you have hundred percent cable  coverage we have the best service all  over europe  so i think infrastructure competition is  one key for get a quality get better  quality and broadband and when  competition works we could phase out of  regulation this is already embedded in  the existing regulatory framework that  could be a little bit enhanced for next  generation networks where you look  market by market but i think there is no  longer a justification for regulation if  this infrastructure competition has so  fantastic results for the consumer yes  now you're finished with the magic word  the consumer there sometimes we forget  about the consumer what do you think  matters most to them at the moment  i think consumers want to have a  seamless experience and they want to  have a whole bundle of different  services uh at a very reasonable price  and that's what cable operators can  offer you know we have embedded wi-fi  systems we have embedded even content  even high quality content in a triple  play bundle you know so it's important  not to think only about infrastructure  it's important to think about the  services and what we see right now is we  are running ahead of the demand you know  so we would like the politicians and the  regulator and the eu commission to look  more on the side of the demand what can  we do to stimulate more the demand of  new services and that's a holistic view  i think for digital europe  and we will deliver i'm sure we will  deliver the best infrastructure  broadband solution  which is technically feasible yes i mean  talking about the eu what do you think  is the one single thing they could do to  help sort of deliver on this digital  single market  besides the demand stimulation and  besides thinking about it  i think they the the regulation which  they do should enable investment i think  that's a key issue because public money  public funding never can do the job you  know most of the money is coming from  private companies and from private  investors  and they are very sensible on changes in  the regulatory framework so what we  should do is to look on these private  investors who by the way have delivered  a great job in the past it's not that  they have done nothing you know they  have delivered very good quality and  listen a little bit more what these  investors want and then in the end of  the day the investors listen also on the  demand of the customers you know so  if you want to have a good business in a  competitive environment  there is no way that you have to listen  to your customer and to support the  needs of the customer so this is a  healthy environment and i think we  should learn out of this healthy  environment and not distorting it that's  the main my main message to the  commission  thank you very much for talking to us  thank you brad
 

